        "resultMode": "none",
        "content": "## SPIKES: six-step protocol for delivering bad news\nEach domain scored out of 5.\n\n## Scorecard\n| Domain | Score |\n| --- | --- |\n| S · Setting up the interview | 3/5 · Good |\n| P · Assessing the patient's Perception | 4/5 · Very good |\n| I · Obtaining the patient's Invitation | 2/5 · Fair |\n| K · Giving Knowledge and information | 3/5 · Good |\n| E · Addressing Emotions | 3/5 · Good |\n| S · Strategy and Summary | 3/5 · Good |\n\n### S · Setting up the interview: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"Hello, Ms. Johnson. I'm Dr. Havelot. I'm the pediatric ICU fellow. ... thank you so much for sitting down with us\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner opened with a clear self-introduction by name and role and acknowledged the family's time and the team's presence, which anchors the setting and establishes engagement: enough for Good. Involving significant others arose organically later (turn 17), but at the outset the learner did not confirm privacy, ask whether the mother wanted anyone present at the start, or flag any time constraints, and physical arrangements cannot be judged in this modality. Those gaps cap it below Very good.\n- **Recommendations:** Early on, orient the family to the plan for the meeting and proactively ask who they would like present before the news unfolds. Suggested wording: \"Before we get into things, is there anyone you'd like with you for this, and do we have enough privacy and time to talk freely right now?\"\n\n### P · Assessing the patient's Perception: 4/5 · Very good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"do you mind just telling me what, why that was so concerning to you, what you're most worried about?\"\n- **Rationale:** Before delivering any news, the learner used an open-ended question to surface what the mother was most worried about, which elicited her specific fears that the team was giving up and that Eli was dying. This directly shaped the subsequent framing. It falls short of Excellent because the question focused on her worry rather than also eliciting her factual understanding of Eli's current medical status, which would have revealed any misinformation to correct.\n- **Recommendations:** Pair the worry question with a perception check of the medical facts, so you learn both the emotion and the understanding. Suggested wording: \"Before I share what we're seeing, can you tell me your understanding of how Eli has been doing on this admission?\"\n\n### I · Obtaining the patient's Invitation: 2/5 · Fair\n- **Evidence:** No corresponding moment in the encounter.\n- **Rationale:** The learner never explicitly asked how much detail the mother wanted or how she preferred to receive the information before launching into the prognostic discussion. The mother's own statements requesting honesty signaled a desire to know, which the learner reasonably responded to, but there was no deliberate invitation step, so this remains a minimal, implicit attempt.\n- **Recommendations:** Ask directly how much detail the family wants and how they'd like it delivered before giving the news. Suggested wording: \"Some families want every detail and some prefer the big picture first. How would you like me to walk you through this?\"\n\n### K · Giving Knowledge and information: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"his escalating ventilator settings and his worsening, uh, respiratory function on this admission is not necessarily a acute reversible process, and this could actually represent a new baseline for him\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner gave information in plain, understandable terms, delivered it in chunks across several turns, and corrected the common palliative-care misconception clearly and reassuringly. This is competent and reaches Good. However, there was no clear warning shot before the core bad news, and the learner never explicitly checked the mother's understanding of what she had heard: relying on her questions to gauge comprehension rather than actively confirming it. Some phrasing was also layered with qualifiers that slightly diffused the message.\n- **Recommendations:** Precede the central news with a brief warning shot and check back on what the family has taken away. Suggested wording: \"I'm afraid I have some difficult news about Eli's lungs. Before I continue, can you tell me what you've taken from what I've said so far?\"\n\n### E · Addressing Emotions: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"That's a very common misconception, and I can completely understand why that would be scary ... it's not just end-of-life care, but it is quality-of-life care\"\n  Character said: \"I haven't been able to breathe since\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner validated the mother's fear about palliative care and repeatedly affirmed that no one was giving up on Eli, which addressed her central emotional concern and was received well. This earns Good. It does not reach Very good because the learner tended to move quickly into reassurance and information rather than first naming the emotion and sitting with it; when the mother said she could not breathe and feared Eli was dying, the response pivoted to content rather than explicitly acknowledging her fear and giving space.\n- **Recommendations:** Name the emotion and pause before offering reassurance or facts. Suggested wording: \"I can hear how frightened you are, and that makes complete sense. Let's take a moment with that before we go further.\"\n\n### S · Strategy and Summary: 3/5 · Good\n- **Evidence:** Learner said: \"I do think he can go home. It may be that he needs to go to a transitional facility in between ... home for him after this admission will likely involve more care and more support in your home\"\n- **Rationale:** The learner oriented the plan around the mother's stated goals of comfort and home, discussed realistic options including a transitional facility and the added home-care needs, framed achievable hope, and arranged a concrete follow-up meeting including the grandmother. This is a solid, goal-concordant close worth Good. It stops short of Very good because there was no explicit check of readiness to discuss next steps, no concise summary of the decisions reached, and the treatment-escalation choices were left somewhat open rather than structured into a shared plan.\n- **Recommendations:** Close with a brief summary of what was agreed and an explicit check that the family is ready to move to planning. Suggested wording: \"To make sure we're on the same page: we're planning for home with more support, with palliative care alongside us. Does it feel okay to talk through the next steps now?\"\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths**: The learner led with the family's worries before disclosing prognosis, used plain language, and handled the palliative-care fear with a clear, reassuring correction. The repeated, genuine framing that no one was giving up on Eli landed well, and the follow-up including the grandmother was handled responsively.\n- **Priority gaps**: There was no deliberate invitation step and no warning shot before the core news, and emotional moments were met with reassurance or information before the feeling itself was named and given space. The close lacked an explicit readiness check and a crisp summary of what was decided.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Add an explicit invitation step: ask how much detail the family wants before disclosing.\n  2. Use a brief warning shot, then name and sit with emotion before pivoting to facts.\n  3. End with a short summary of decisions and an explicit readiness check before planning.\n",
