        "resultMode": "none",
        "content": "## ICS4: Communication Around Serious Illness, Including End-of-Life Care: Level 4 / 5 · Proficient\nPediatric Critical Care milestone · levels 1-5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 4 · Proficient: the highest level fully demonstrated; the ceiling at 4 is set by the simulation (Level 5 not assessable here), not by a learner shortfall.\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1-4 demonstrated; Level 5 not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity).\n\n## Evidence by level\n\n### Level 1 · Novice: Demonstrated\nThe learner treats prognostic communication as the foundation of the shared decision about Eli's care, explicitly connecting an honest prognosis to aligning treatment with the family's wishes.\nLearner said: \"we just wanted to make sure that we understood what you wanted for Eli in terms of hisquality of life\"\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner: Demonstrated\nBefore delivering any prognostic content, the learner assesses the mother's existing awareness and fears, inviting her to name her concern so information can be tailored to it.\nLearner said: \"before we start, c- do you mind just telling me what, why that was so concerning to you, what you're most worried about\"\n\n### Level 3 · Competent: Demonstrated\nThe learner delivers concrete prognostic information: that the respiratory decline may not be reversible and may represent a new, more-supported baseline: and responds to the mother's distress rather than ignoring it.\nLearner said: \"his worsening, uh, respiratory function on this admission is not necessarily a acute reversible process, and this could actually represent a new baseline for him\"\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient: Demonstrated\nThe learner tailors the prognosis to Eli's chronic trajectory (already vent-dependent, G-tube), names medical uncertainty honestly rather than overpromising, and repeatedly ties the discussion back to the family's stated goals of comfort and home while attending to emotion (reframing palliative care when it frightened the mother).\nLearner said: \"in terms of whether this is more than his body can take, um, you know, I, I can't say that for sure. I'm just being honest with you ... if you feel like we are doing more than you would wish for us to do for him, that's completely appropriate\"\n\n### Level 5 · Expert: Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nLevel 5 requires coaching others in communicating prognosis. This encounter placed the learner alone with a single family member; no trainee or colleague was present to coach, so the behavior had no occasion to arise. It does not count against the learner.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner met every behavior through Level 4. They framed the meeting's purpose as aligning care with what the family wants (Level 1), and before delivering news they probed the mother's worry and prognostic awareness (Level 2). They then delivered specific, honest prognostic information: that worsening ventilator requirements may represent an irreversible new baseline: and consistently named and validated the mother's fear that the team was giving up (Level 3). At Level 4 the learner tailored the message to Eli's chronic, escalating disease trajectory, named uncertainty plainly rather than falsely reassuring, corrected the misconception that palliative care means abandonment, and anchored next steps in the family's own goals of comfort and home.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 5 is defined by coaching others in prognostic communication. The assessment holds at Level 4 because the scenario: a one-on-one family meeting with no learner or peer to supervise: gave no opportunity to demonstrate coaching (no opportunity), not because the learner fell short.\n\n## Recommendations\nThe learner is practicing at a proficient level for direct serious-illness communication. Two refinements would strengthen it further: name emotion more explicitly before pivoting to information (a brief acknowledgment that the word frightened her), and consider offering a gentle recommendation anchored in the family's values rather than leaving the decision open, which can relieve decisional burden. Suggested wording: \"Based on what you've told me matters most: keeping him comfortable and getting him home: my recommendation would be to focus our efforts there, and I'll help you think through what that looks like.\" To grow toward Level 5, seek opportunities to debrief and coach junior trainees after family meetings.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths**: The learner opened by eliciting the mother's fears before delivering news, gave an honest, trajectory-specific prognosis without sugarcoating, validated the repeated worry about giving up, and skillfully reframed palliative care. They consistently returned to the family's goals of comfort and home and accommodated the request to include the grandmother.\n- **Priority gaps**: Emotion was addressed mostly by reassurance rather than explicit naming; the learner could more actively offer a values-based recommendation to ease the decision. The highest level (coaching others) could not be surfaced by this one-on-one format.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Explicitly name and acknowledge emotion before moving to information.\n  2. Offer a gentle, values-anchored recommendation rather than leaving options fully open.\n  3. Seek supervised teaching opportunities to coach junior learners on prognostic conversations toward Level 5.\n",
